{"name":"Tangerine Yellow","hex":"#fecd01","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tangerine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A slightly ovoid, orange-coloured  citrus fruit with a rough peel and a sour-sweet taste which is larger than a clementine and sometimes classed as a variety of mandarin orange.","A deep yellowish-orange colour, like that of a tangerine fruit."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a deep yellowish-orange colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tangerine"},{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"}],"entries":[{"title":"School bus yellow","blocks":[{"text":"School bus yellow is a color that was specifically formulated for use on school buses in North America in 1939. Originally officially named National School Bus Chrome, the color is now officially known in Canada and the United States as National School Bus Glossy Yellow."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/School_bus_yellow","wikidata":"Q987293"},{"title":"Tangerine","blocks":[{"text":"The tangerine is a type of citrus fruit that is orange in colour, that is considered either a variety of the mandarin orange, or a closely related species, under the name Citrus tangerina, or yet as a hybrid of mandarin orange varieties, with some pomelo contribution."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/TangerineFruit.jpg/330px-TangerineFruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangerine","wikidata":"Q516494"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow","wikidata":"Q943"},{"title":"Tangerine (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color tangerine is a tone of orange named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/TangerineFruit.jpg/330px-TangerineFruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangerine_(color)","wikidata":"Q846504"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/2008_blue_bird_vision.jpg/120px-2008_blue_bird_vision.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/2008_blue_bird_vision.jpg/250px-2008_blue_bird_vision.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/th